## Agent handover and recovery

Before handing work to another agent, ending a meaningful checkpoint, or approaching a context/quota interruption, leave enough durable state to resume without reconstructing the conversation.

Required handover evidence:

- exact repository/worktree path, branch and remote;
- current issue and dependency gate;
- last pushed implementation/evidence SHA;
- working-tree state, including every uncommitted file and its owner;
- current TDD phase (`RED`, `GREEN`, `REFACTOR`, or `N/A`) and the last relevant command/result;
- exact next action, expected files and known blockers/risks;
- dev-diary link or an explicit note that it still must be published.

For the v2 narrative branch, maintain `docs/v2.0.0/implementation-handover.md` as the branch-local recovery record. Update it before every meaningful push, ownership transfer, long pause, or planned agent replacement. The GitHub issue/dev diary remains authoritative for accepted progress and immutable pushed SHAs.

The receiving agent must verify `pwd`, `git status --short --branch`, `git rev-parse HEAD` and the latest issue diary before editing. If the path, branch, SHA or dirty-file ownership differs from the handover, stop and reconcile it; never discard or absorb unexplained changes.

One file cluster has one editing owner at a time. A verifier is read-only. An agent may commit, push, close an issue, create a PR or mutate external state only when the user has authorized that action.

Quota/model failover is not assumed. Durability comes from small pushed checkpoints, issue diary evidence and this recovery record; an in-memory agent message alone is not a handover.
